(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-childcare"]={"plain":{"bills":{"childcare":{"slug":"childcare","title":"The Child Care and Pre-K Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title.","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the \"Child Care and Pre-K Act.\""},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings.","part":null,"body":"Congress finds as follows:\n\n1. The Child Care and Development Block Grant Act of 1990 reaches roughly one in seven of the children its own eligibility rules describe. Its authorization of appropriations expired at the end of fiscal year 2020; its payments to States are \"subject to the availability of appropriations\"; and the Act states that nothing in it entitles any family to any benefit. Every two years the Comptroller General counts the families on its waiting lists.\n2. The cost of care for a child under five exceeds the cost of in-State public university tuition in most States, and the share of family income it consumes is highest for the families with the least. The Department of Health and Human Services treated 7 percent of family income as the benchmark of affordable child care from 2016 until it rescinded the benchmark effective July 13, 2026, and no Federal statute has ever adopted it.\n3. Where a jurisdiction has made pre-kindergarten universal and paid its teachers on the public-school scale, the effects have persisted into secondary school and beyond; where a jurisdiction has scaled seats quickly without paying the workforce, the measured effects on children were null or negative. The difference between the two records is the compensation and stability of the adults in the room.\n4. Child care workers are paid less than most other occupations that require comparable skill, and the turnover that follows is the mechanism by which quality is lost. The payment rates under existing law are derived from surveys of the prices charged in a market whose prices are set by what families can pay, and a rate set from that survey cannot fund the wage that stability requires.\n5. Existing law pays providers for attendance rather than enrollment, so a provider's revenue falls when a child is sick and the provider's costs do not. That practice, together with rate setting from prices, is why infant and toddler care is structurally underpaid and structurally scarce.\n6. The Department of Defense has for three decades run a child care system for military families in which fees are set as a share of family income, providers are paid the cost of quality, and the workforce is paid on a published scale with Federal benefits. It is the largest employer-sponsored child care system in the country, and its accreditation rates and staff retention exceed those of the civilian sector. This Act extends its model to every family.\n7. Where maternal labor-force participation has been measured after a universal program began, it rose substantially. The revenue that follows is real, is not counted in the estimates on which this Act rests, and does not need to be for the Act to be justified.\n8. A program that depends on an annual appropriation, on a State's decision to participate, or on an officer's decision to approve a plan can be ended by any of them without a vote to end it. Each obligation in this Act is therefore stated so that it operates without any such decision, and each amount necessary to it is appropriated permanently.\n\n9. Every State provides a free public education to the children who reside in it, and has done so for the whole of living memory without inquiring into a child's immigration status. The years before kindergarten are the years in which the difference between children is made, and a program that begins at five arrives after the gap it is meant to close has opened. This Act extends to the years before school the rule that has governed school itself: **a child who lives here is taught here.**"},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions.","part":null,"body":"In this Act:\n\n**(a)** \"The 1990 Act\" means the Child Care and Development Block Grant Act of 1990 (42 U.S.C. 9857 et seq.), and a reference to a section of the 1990 Act by its number in title 42, United States Code, is a reference to that section as amended by this Act.\n\n**(b)** \"Covered child\" means an individual who resides in a State and has not entered kindergarten, without regard to the individual's age, family income, immigration status, or the employment, training, or educational status of any parent; an individual remains a covered child until the first day of the school year in which the individual is first eligible to enroll in kindergarten under the law of the State of residence.\n\n**(c)** \"Parent\" has the meaning given in section 9858n(10) of title 42, United States Code.\n\n**(d)** \"Family\" means a covered child and the parents and other individuals whose income is counted in the verified income record of the household in which the child resides; where no such record exists, the individuals who would be so counted.\n\n**(e)** \"Family income\" means, for a month, the income stated in the verified income record for the family, annualized; and where no verified income record exists, the family's attested income for the month, annualized.\n\n**(f)** \"Verified income record\" means the record established under section 1-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act, and where that Act is not in effect, a determination of family income by the lead agency under the procedures the lead agency applies under section 9858c(c)(2)(N) of title 42, United States Code.\n\n**(g)** \"State median income\" means, for a family of a given size, the State median income for a family of that size as published by the Secretary for purposes of the 1990 Act.\n\n**(h)** \"Lead agency\" has the meaning given in section 9858n(9) of title 42, United States Code, and includes the Secretary, or a local lead agency designated under section 5-5, wherever the Secretary carries out this Act under that section.\n\n**(i)** \"Secretary\" means the Secretary of Health and Human Services.\n\n**(j)** \"Eligible child care provider\" has the meaning given in section 9858n(6) of title 42, United States Code, and includes a local educational agency, a public school, and a Head Start agency designated under section 641 of the Head Start Act (42 U.S.C. 9836) as to the care and early education it provides.\n\n**(k)** \"Participating provider\" means an eligible child care provider that has entered into and is in compliance with a participation agreement under section 3-2.\n\n**(l)** \"Cost-model rate\" means the rate determined under section 1-4 for a class of care.\n\n**(m)** \"Family copay\" means the amount determined under section 1-3.\n\n**(n)** \"Full-time care\" means child care services for the number of hours per week set in the schedule, or such greater number of hours as the parent's work, training, or educational schedule requires, as attested by the parent; and includes care during non-standard hours where the parent so attests.\n\n**(o)** \"Simultaneous children\" means, for a family and a month, the covered children of the family enrolled with a participating provider in that month, counting the children of a single multiple birth as one child.\n\n**(p)** \"Child care staff member\" has the meaning given in section 9858f(i)(2) of title 42, United States Code, and includes an owner-operator of a family child care home as to the care that individual provides.\n\n**(q)** \"Parity compensation\" means the compensation determined under sections 4-1 and 4-2 for a role.\n\n**(r)** \"Local educational agency\" and \"elementary school\" have the meanings given in section 8101 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act of 1965 (20 U.S.C. 7801).\n\n**(s)** \"State\" has the meaning given in section 9858n(14) of title 42, United States Code, and, wherever a provision of this Act funds, protects, or recognizes, includes each Indian tribe and tribal organization carrying out a program under section 9858m(c) of that title; a provision of this Act binds a Tribal government only as to a function the Tribe has voluntarily assumed under that section or by contract or compact, and the omission of any further application to Tribal law is a determination by Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(u)** \"Child care services\" means the care, supervision, and early education of a child by a person other than the child's parent, for compensation, in any setting and at any hours, and includes a pre-kindergarten program under title II.\n\n**(v)** \"Place\" means the capacity to enroll one child, within the licensed or registered capacity of a participating provider for the age of the child, and \"places furnished\" means the children enrolled.\n\n**(w)** \"Enrolled\" means, as to a child and a participating provider, that the parent has selected the provider under section 1-1(b) and the provider has agreed to furnish care from a stated date, and enrollment continues until ended under section 1-1(e).\n\n**(x)** \"Resides\" means that the child is living in the State, as the parent attests; no durational requirement, and no requirement of domicile, lawful presence, or a fixed address, applies, and a child experiencing homelessness resides where the child is living.\n\n**(y)** \"Locality\" means a county, or where the State plan so provides a smaller area the plan names, and where the Secretary carries out this Act under section 5-5, a county.\n\n**(t)** \"Middle-Class Growth Rate\" means the rate certified under section 1-3 of the People's Prosperity Act, and where that Act is not in effect, the percentage change in the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics from the month 12 months before the most recent month for which it is published before the first day of the period to which the rate is applied to that most recent month, using each month's value as first published."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The entitlement.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858d(a) of title 42, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(a) The child care guarantee**\n>\n> **(1) Entitlement.** Every covered child is entitled to full-time child care services under this subchapter, furnished by a participating provider selected by the child's parent, for every month in which the child is a covered child, at a family copay not exceeding the amount determined under section 9858c(c)(5) of this title. The entitlement is an entitlement of the child, enforceable by the child's parent, and arises by operation of this subsection on the date the parent selects a participating provider.**\n>\n> **(2) No condition.** The entitlement under paragraph (1) does not depend on the employment, training, or educational status of any parent; on family income; on the availability of appropriations; on the approval of any State plan; on any election, waiver, or determination by any officer; or on the child's or any family member's citizenship or immigration status. The omission from this paragraph of any further condition is a determination by Congress that the condition does not apply.**\n>\n> **(3) Children not covered children.** An eligible child under section 9858n(4) of this title who is not a covered child is entitled to assistance under this subchapter on the terms of that section, at a family copay not exceeding the amount determined under section 9858c(c)(5) of this title for the family, from the amounts allotted under section 9858m of this title.**\n>\n> **(4) Providers.** Nothing in this subchapter entitles any child care provider to a contract or grant; a participating provider is entitled to payment under section 9858h(d) of this title for each covered child enrolled with it.**\n\n**(a-1) What the replaced subsection contained, and what becomes of it.** Section 9858d(a) as it read before this amendment had two paragraphs. **Paragraph (1)** \u2014 that nothing in the subchapter entitles a provider or a certificate holder \"to any contract, grant or benefit\" \u2014 **is carried forward** as to providers at new paragraph (4), and is displaced as to children, which is the point of the amendment. **Paragraph (2)** \u2014 that nothing in the subchapter limits \"the right of any State to impose additional limitations or conditions on contracts or grants funded under this subchapter\" \u2014 **is repealed as to a covered child and preserved otherwise.** A State may not condition the entitlement under paragraph (1), because \u00a7 3-2(a) of this Act makes the conditions of participation exclusive and paragraph (2) of the new subsection says the entitlement depends on no State election or determination. **For an eligible child who is not a covered child**, whose assistance continues under paragraph (3) on the terms of section 9858n(4), **the State's latitude is unchanged**, and nothing in this Act narrows it.\n\n**(b) Selection.** A parent selects a participating provider by notifying the provider or the lead agency; no application, interview, documentation, or other act by the parent is required beyond the identity of the child and parent and the child's residence in the State. Care begins on the date the parent and provider agree, and payment under section 9858h(d) of title 42, United States Code, runs from that date. A child is enrolled with one participating provider at a time, except that a parent may enroll a child with two providers for hours that do not overlap, not exceeding full-time care in total, and each is paid for the hours it furnishes.\n\n**(c) Verification; correction one way.** The lead agency shall verify the child's age and residence within the period set in the schedule, from the verified income record and from records the State holds, without requiring any document from the parent that a record the State holds would supply. An enrollment found not to satisfy subsection (a) is ended prospectively, on notice, from the first day of the month after the finding; no amount paid for care furnished before that day is recoverable from the family or the provider, and no inference is drawn from the error.\n\n**(d) Full-time care; schedule chosen by the parent.** The parent selects the schedule of care up to full-time care. A lead agency or provider may not condition the hours of care on the parent's work schedule, require the parent to demonstrate a need for the hours selected, or reduce the hours of care for a reason other than the parent's selection.\n\n**(e) Continuity.** A covered child's enrollment continues until the parent ends it, the child ceases to be a covered child, or the provider ceases to be a participating provider. A provider may not end a covered child's enrollment except on notice for the period set in the schedule and on a ground stated in the participation agreement under section 3-2(b)(7); non-payment of the family copay is not such a ground.\n\n**(f) Definitions carried into the 1990 Act.** Section 9858n of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(16) Covered child; participating provider; family copay; cost-model rate; full-time care.** The terms \"covered child,\" \"participating provider,\" \"family copay,\" \"cost-model rate,\" and \"full-time care\" have the meanings given in section 0-3 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"*Amends the Child Care and Development Block Grant Act of 1990, 42 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 9857\u20139858r.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Eligibility determined on the record.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858c(c)(2)(N) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(v) Covered children.** For a covered child, the State shall determine age, residence, and family income on the basis of the verified income record and the composition facts stated in it, under section 4-1 of the Keep Your Benefits Act, and where a fact is not stated in that record, on the parent's attestation. No redetermination of a covered child's eligibility is made; the family copay is redetermined under section 9858c(c)(5)(E) of this title.**\n\n**(b) Attestation.** A parent's attestation of a fact this Act requires is sufficient unless and until the lead agency establishes, from a record it holds, that the fact is otherwise; the lead agency shall not require documentation of a fact a record it holds would supply."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. The family copay.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858c(c)(5) of title 42, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(5) Family copay**\n>\n> ~~The State plan shall provide that the State will establish and periodically revise, by rule, a sliding fee scale that provides for cost sharing (that is not a barrier to families receiving assistance under this subchapter) by the families that receive child care services for which assistance is provided under this subchapter.~~\n>\n> **(A) Amount.** The family copay for a family for a month is the lesser of \u2014\n>\n> **(i) the applicable percentage of the amount by which the family's income for the month, annualized, exceeds the copay floor, divided by 12; and**\n>\n> **(ii) the aggregate of the cost-model rates for the family's children enrolled with participating providers in the month, other than the rates for places under title II of the Child Care and Pre-K Act.**\n>\n> **(B) Applicable percentage.** The applicable percentage is the sum of \u2014**\n>\n> **(i) the base percentage set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act, multiplied by the seat share of the family's first simultaneous child;**\n>\n> **(ii) the second-child increment set in that schedule, multiplied by the seat share of the second; and**\n>\n> **(iii) the additional-child increment set in that schedule, multiplied by the seat share of each simultaneous child beyond the second.**\n>\n> **The simultaneous children are ranked in descending order of seat share. The children of a single multiple birth are one child for purposes of this subparagraph, and that child's seat share is the greatest of theirs.**\n>\n> **A child's seat share for a month is the weekly hours of enrollment, as fixed under paragraph (4)(B), of every class of care in which the child is enrolled with a participating provider, including a place under title II of the Child Care and Pre-K Act and including each provider's class where the child is enrolled with two providers under section 1-1(b) of that Act, less the weekly hours of enrollment of the child's place under that title, each averaged over the weeks of the month, divided by the weekly hours of full-time care set in that schedule. A seat share is not less than zero and not more than one.**\n>\n> **(C) Copay floor.** The copay floor is the percentage of State median income for a family of the same size set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act.**\n>\n> **(D) No minimum; no other charge.** There is no minimum family copay. The family copay is the only amount a family may be required to pay for child care services under this subchapter, and section 3-2(b)(1) of the Child Care and Pre-K Act governs every other charge.**\n>\n> **(E) Redetermination one way.** The family copay is redetermined on the annual date set in the State plan from the verified income record as it then stands. A parent may at any time report a reduction in family income, and the family copay is reduced from the first day of the month in which the report is made. An increase in family income takes effect only at the annual redetermination. No amount is recoverable from a family on account of a family copay determined from a verified income record, attestation, or enrollment report later found to be inaccurate.**\n>\n> **(F) Pre-kindergarten hours.** The family copay does not apply to hours of care furnished under title II of the Child Care and Pre-K Act.**\n>\n> **(G) Collection.** The family copay is owed to the lead agency, which shall bill the family monthly and shall pay each participating provider the cost-model rate without reduction for the family copay. The lead agency may collect an unpaid family copay by any means available for the collection of a debt owed to the State, including offset against a refund of State tax, and may bill and collect through an agent, including the participating provider; a provider acting as agent bears no liability for any amount not collected, and no unpaid family copay is a ground for ending or suspending the enrollment of any child or for any reduction of any payment to a provider.**\n\n**(b) Conforming.** Section 9858n(13) of title 42, United States Code, is repealed, and section 9858c(c)(3)(B)(i) of that title is amended by striking \"on a sliding fee scale basis\" and inserting \"at the family copay under paragraph (5)\"."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Cost-model rates.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> ~~**(A) In general.** The State plan shall certify that payment rates for the provision of child care services for which assistance is provided in accordance with this subchapter are **sufficient to ensure equal access** for eligible children to child care services that are comparable to child care services in the State or substate area involved that are provided to children whose parents are not eligible to receive assistance under this subchapter or to receive child care assistance under any other Federal or State program, and shall provide a summary of the facts relied on by the State to determine that such rates are sufficient to ensure such access.~~\n>\n> ~~**(B) Survey.** The State plan shall \u2014 (i) demonstrate that the State has ... developed and conducted ... **a statistically valid and reliable survey of the market rates for child care services in the State** ... or an alternative methodology, such as a cost estimation model ...; (ii) demonstrate that the State prepared a detailed report containing the results ...; (iii) describe how the State will set payment rates ... in accordance with the results of the market rates survey ...; and (iv) describe how the State will provide for timely payment for child care services.~~\n>\n> ~~**(C) Construction.** (i) **No private right of action.** Nothing in this paragraph shall be construed to create a private right of action if the State acted in accordance with this paragraph. (ii) **No prohibition of certain different rates.** Nothing in this subchapter shall be construed to prevent a State from differentiating the payment rates described in subparagraph (B)(iii) on the basis of such factors as \u2014 (I) geographic location ...; (II) the age or particular needs of children ...; (III) whether the providers provide child care services during weekend and other nontraditional hours; or (IV) the State's determination that such differentiated payment rates may enable a parent to choose high-quality child care that best fits the parent's needs.~~\n\n> **(4) Payment rates**\n>\n> **(A) Cost-model rate.** The State plan shall set the payment rate for each class of child care services for which assistance is provided under this subchapter at the cost of furnishing that class of care at the standards in effect under paragraph (2)(H) and (2)(I), computed by a cost model whose inputs are \u2014**\n>\n> **(i) parity compensation under sections 4-1 and 4-2 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act for each role, at the group sizes and child-to-staff ratios in effect under paragraph (2)(H) for the age of the children in the class, for the hours of operation of the class;**\n>\n> **(ii) the cost of substitute staff, planning time, and professional development at the levels set in the schedule to that Act;**\n>\n> **(iii) the cost of facilities, including rent or its equivalent, utilities, maintenance, and insurance, by locality;**\n>\n> **(iv) the cost of food, supplies, equipment, transportation where furnished, and administration, at the percentages of the amounts under clauses (i) through (iii) set in that schedule; and**\n>\n> **(v) an enrollment factor equal to the reasonable enrollment set in that schedule, so that the rate covers the costs under clauses (i) through (iv) at that enrollment.**\n>\n> **(B) Classes.** The State plan shall set a rate for each combination of provider setting, age of child, hours of operation, weekly hours of enrollment, and locality, and shall set rates for non-standard hours, for children with disabilities, and for a family child care home at the enrollment mix the home actually has, so that a home serving no children aged three or four is paid the cost of the care it furnishes. The weekly hours of enrollment of a child's class are the provider's weekly hours of operation for the child's place, less each hour that falls within a unit of that place the provider offers for enrollment to another child; a parent's election not to use hours within the class, including by late arrival or early departure, does not reduce them. Where neither the plan nor the Secretary under section 5-5(d) of the Child Care and Pre-K Act has set a rate for the weekly hours of enrollment of a class a participating provider offers, the rate for that class is the rate for the class of the next greater weekly hours of enrollment, otherwise identical, set by the plan or, where the plan sets none, by the Secretary, multiplied by the ratio of the hours offered to those hours.**\n>\n> **(C) Not from prices.** A rate under this paragraph shall not be derived from, limited by, or adjusted to a survey of the prices charged for child care services, and the cost model shall not include any input the plan does not name.**\n>\n> **(D) Publication; annual update.** The lead agency shall publish the cost model, every input and its source, and every rate, and shall update every input annually on the date set in the State plan. Where an input is a compensation amount under section 4-1 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act, the update takes effect on the date the corresponding public-school schedule changes. A rate not updated by the date set in the plan is, from that date, the prior rate increased by the Middle-Class Growth Rate, and no rate shall be reduced by an update except on a published change in an input under subparagraph (A)(iii) or (iv).**\n>\n> **(E) Timeliness.** A participating provider shall be paid for each month within the period set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act after the end of the month, and an amount not paid within that period bears interest at the rate under section 6621 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 from the day it was due.**\n>\n> **(F) Differentiation.** Nothing in this subchapter prevents a State from setting a rate above the cost-model rate for a class of care.**\n\n**(b) Where no rate has been published.** Until a State publishes a rate under section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code, for a class of care, the rate for that class is the rate the Secretary publishes under section 5-5(d) for that class in that State, and a participating provider is entitled to it."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Payment on enrollment.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858c(c)(2)(S) of title 42, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(S) Payment on enrollment**\n>\n> ~~The plan shall include \u2014 (i) a certification that the payment practices of child care providers in the State that serve children who receive assistance under this subchapter reflect generally accepted payment practices of child care providers in the State that serve children who do not receive assistance under this subchapter, so as to provide stability of funding and encourage more child care providers to serve children who receive assistance under this subchapter; and (ii) an assurance that the State will, to the extent practicable, implement enrollment and eligibility policies that support the fixed costs of providing child care services by delinking provider reimbursement rates from an eligible child's occasional absences due to holidays or unforseen circumstances such as illness.~~\n>\n> **(i) Enrollment.** A participating provider is paid the cost-model rate for each child enrolled with it for each month of enrollment, without regard to the child's attendance, from the amounts under section 9858h(d) of this title.**\n>\n> **(ii) Verification.** Where a child has not attended for the number of consecutive days set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act, the provider shall so notify the lead agency, and the lead agency shall verify with the parent whether the enrollment continues. Payment continues during verification. An enrollment the parent confirms continues; an enrollment the parent does not confirm within the period set in that schedule ends at the end of that period.**\n>\n> **(iii) Recovery.** An amount paid for a child who was not enrolled, for a month after an enrollment ended, or for a class of more weekly hours of enrollment than the child's class under paragraph (4)(B), is recoverable from the provider, with interest, and from no other person. A provider that reported an enrollment it knew did not exist, or a class it knew exceeded the child's weekly hours of enrollment, is ineligible to participate for the period set in that schedule. No amount is recoverable from a family under this clause.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Appropriation and payment.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Appropriation.** Section 9858 of title 42, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\u00a7 9858. Appropriations**\n>\n> ~~There is authorized to be appropriated to carry out this subchapter $2,360,000,000 for fiscal year 2015, $2,478,000,000 for fiscal year 2016, $2,539,950,000 for fiscal year 2017, $2,603,448,750 for fiscal year 2018, $2,668,534,969 for fiscal year 2019, and $2,748,591,018 for fiscal year 2020.~~\n>\n> **(a) The guarantee.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary to make the payments required by section 9858h(d) of this title and by titles II through V of the Child Care and Pre-K Act. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, do not lapse, and are not subject to apportionment delay or to any programmatic withholding.**\n>\n> **(b) The block grant.** There are appropriated to carry out section 9858m of this title, for each fiscal year, an amount equal to the amount appropriated to carry out this subchapter for the fiscal year in which the Child Care and Pre-K Act is enacted, increased for each subsequent fiscal year by the Middle-Class Growth Rate. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently.**\n\n**(b) Payment.** Section 9858h of title 42, United States Code, is amended \u2014\n\n1. in subsection (a), by striking \"Subject to the availability of appropriations, a State\" and inserting \"A State\"; and\n2. by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(d) Payments for covered children.** The Secretary shall pay to each State, for each month, an amount equal to the Federal share set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act of the aggregate of the cost-model rates payable under section 9858c(c)(2)(S) of this title for the covered children enrolled with participating providers in the State in that month, together with the amounts payable under section 9858c(c)(4)(E) of this title. The payment is made from the appropriation under section 9858(a) of this title, in advance on the State's estimate for the month and reconciled on the State's report for the month, and is not subject to any allotment, formula, reservation, or ceiling under section 9858m of this title. The State share is the remainder, and section 5-4 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act governs its source.**\n\n**(c) Tribal programs.** Section 9858m(c) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(7) Covered children.** An Indian tribe or tribal organization carrying out a program under this subsection shall be paid under section 9858h(d) of this title for each covered child enrolled with a participating provider under the program, at the cost-model rate the tribe or organization sets under the standards developed under paragraph (2)(D), and the Federal share for such a payment is 100 percent. Paragraph (5) applies to a covered child.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. Conforming amendments to the 1990 Act and to section 9858r.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Purposes.** Section 9857(b) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(8) to guarantee to every child who has not entered kindergarten child care at a family copay that is a share of family income above a floor, scaled to the hours of the place the child holds, and to pay for it at the cost of furnishing it.**\"\n\n**(b) Waiver restriction.** Section 9858g(c)(8) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**Nothing in this subsection, and no other provision of this subchapter, authorizes the Secretary or a State to waive, condition, suspend, or modify the entitlement under section 9858d(a) of this title, the family copay under section 9858c(c)(5) of this title, or a cost-model rate under section 9858c(c)(4) of this title, as to any child, family, provider, or State.**\"\n\n**(c) Waiting lists become unmet demand.** Section 9858r of title 42, United States Code, is amended \u2014\n\n1. in the heading, by striking \"Studies on waiting lists\" and inserting \"**Unmet demand**\";\n2. in subsection (a), by striking paragraphs (1) through (3) and inserting: \"**(1) the number of covered children for whom a parent sought a participating provider and no participating provider with a place for the child existed within the access standard set in the schedule to the Child Care and Pre-K Act, by locality, age of child, and hours of care sought; and (2) the number of eligible children who are not covered children and who have applied for and not received assistance under this subchapter**\"; and\n3. in subsection (b), by striking \"every 2 years thereafter\" and inserting \"**annually thereafter, and the report is published on the date it is submitted**\".\n\n**(d) Period of plan.** Section 9858c(b) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by inserting before the period: \"**, and the failure of a State to submit a plan for a period does not suspend the entitlement under section 9858d(a) of this title, which is carried out under section 5-5 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act until a plan is in effect**\".\n\n**(e) Priority language.** Section 9858c(c)(3)(B)(i) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by inserting after \"very low family incomes (taking into consideration family size)\" the following: \"**, which priority applies to the amounts under section 9858m of this title and not to the entitlement under section 9858d(a) of this title**\".\n\n**(f) Sanctions do not reach the guarantee.** Section 9858g(b)(2) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following: \"**(D) The guarantee.** No sanction under this paragraph, and no reimbursement, deduction, recoupment, or disqualification under it, reduces, delays, or conditions a payment under section 9858h(d) of this title, the entitlement of any child under section 9858d(a) of this title, or the payment of any participating provider. Where a State is disqualified under subparagraph (B), the Secretary carries out the Child Care and Pre-K Act in the State under section 5-5 of that Act for the period of the disqualification.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-1-8","heading":"\u00a7 1-8. Phase-in by age.","part":"TITLE I \u2014 THE CHILD CARE GUARANTEE","body":"**(a) Dates.** Section 9858d(a)(1) of title 42, United States Code, as amended by section 1-1, applies to a covered child on the date set in the schedule for the age band in which the child's age falls on that date, and to every child in that band thereafter. A child in a band whose date has arrived remains a covered child without regard to a later birthday.\n\n**(b) The workforce first.** Sections 1-4 and 1-5, and title IV, apply from the first day of the first fiscal year beginning after enactment to every provider furnishing care for which assistance is provided under the 1990 Act, whether or not any child is yet a covered child.\n\n**(c) Where no place exists.** Where, on the date a band takes effect or at any time after, no participating provider has a place for a covered child within the access standard set in the schedule, the child's parent may enroll the child with any eligible child care provider, including a provider described in section 9858n(6)(B) of title 42, United States Code, at the cost-model rate for that class of care, and the lead agency's duty under section 3-6 arises. The absence of a place is not a ground for delaying, suspending, or conditioning the entitlement."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. The pre-kindergarten entitlement.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Entitlement.** Every covered child who has attained the age of three years on the date the State uses to determine eligibility for kindergarten is entitled to a place in a pre-kindergarten program of a participating provider selected by the child's parent, for not less than the daily hours and annual days set in the schedule, at no charge to the family. The entitlement arises by operation of this subsection on the parent's selection and is subject to no condition other than the child's age and residence.\n\n**(b) Wraparound care.** Hours of care for a child described in subsection (a) beyond the hours under that subsection are child care services under section 9858d(a) of title 42, United States Code, at the family copay.\n\n**(c) Payment.** A participating provider is paid for each child under subsection (a) the cost-model rate for pre-kindergarten care for the hours furnished, under section 9858c(c)(2)(S) of title 42, United States Code, and the Federal share of that payment is the pre-kindergarten Federal share set in the schedule.\n\n**(d) Program standard.** A pre-kindergarten program under this title is a program that meets the standards under section 9858c(c)(2)(H) and (I) of title 42, United States Code, for the age of the children served, whose lead teacher meets section 4-3(a), and that implements the State's early learning and developmental guidelines under section 9858c(c)(2)(T) of that title. No other standard, and no assessment of any child, is a condition of a place under this title.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. The State election.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Election.** A State shall elect in its plan under section 9858c of title 42, United States Code, to carry out this title under track A (section 2-3) or track B (section 2-4). A State that has not elected is under track A.\n\n**(b) Lock.** An election is in effect for the number of years set in the schedule from the first day of the fiscal year in which it takes effect, and may not be changed during that period. A change of election takes effect at the start of the first fiscal year beginning after the period.\n\n**(c) Equal obligation.** The election changes nothing under titles I, III, IV, and V. A State under either track is subject to every provision of those titles, to the infant-and-toddler supply standard under section 2-5, and to the same consequence under section 2-5(c) for failing it."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Track A \u2014 mixed delivery.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Providers.** Under track A, a pre-kindergarten place may be furnished by any participating provider that operates a program meeting section 2-1(d), including a local educational agency, a Head Start agency, a licensed center, and a licensed family child care home.\n\n**(b) Community-provider floor.** In each fiscal year, the share of pre-kindergarten places under section 2-1 furnished by participating providers other than local educational agencies shall not be less than the greater of the community-provider floor set in the schedule and the share so furnished in the State in the fiscal year preceding enactment. Where the share falls below the floor, the lead agency shall, until it is restored, contract for new places under this title only with providers other than local educational agencies, and shall publish the shortfall and the places contracted."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Track B \u2014 school-based delivery.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Providers.** Under track B, a pre-kindergarten place is furnished by a local educational agency, directly or under contract with a participating provider, and every provider furnishing a place under such a contract is a participating provider paid the cost-model rate.\n\n**(b) Community providers protected in the rate.** The cost model under section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code, in a track B State shall compute the rate for each participating provider other than a local educational agency at the enrollment mix the provider actually has, so that the fixed costs of a provider that serves no children aged three or four are covered at the reasonable enrollment set in the schedule for the places it does furnish.\n\n**(c) Contracts not exclusive.** A local educational agency in a track B State may not, as a condition of a contract under subsection (a), restrict a participating provider from furnishing care to children of any age under title I."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. The infant-and-toddler supply standard.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Standard.** In each fiscal year, the number of places for children under the age of three furnished by participating providers in the State, per 100 children under the age of three residing in the State, shall not be less than the greater of the number in the fiscal year preceding enactment and the standard set in the schedule for the fiscal year.\n\n**(b) Measurement.** The lead agency shall measure and publish the number under subsection (a) annually, by locality, counting places from the licensing and registration records the State maintains under section 9858c(c)(2)(F) of title 42, United States Code, for participating providers, and children from the Census Bureau's most recent estimate of population. A place counted is one the State has licensed or registered for a child under the age of three; a place the State has not so licensed or registered is not counted.\n\n**(c) Consequence.** In a State that fails the standard under subsection (a) for a fiscal year, until the standard is met: (1) the lead agency shall obligate the supply amounts under section 3-6 only for places for children under the age of three; (2) no new place under section 2-1 may be contracted with a local educational agency; and (3) the Secretary shall carry out section 3-6 directly in the State, from the appropriation under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, for places for children under the age of three. The consequence is the same under either track."},{"anchor":"sec-2-6","heading":"\u00a7 2-6. Head Start.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"**(a) Participating by operation of law.** A Head Start agency designated under section 641 of the Head Start Act (42 U.S.C. 9836), and each of its delegate agencies, is a participating provider under this Act as to the care and early education it furnishes, without a participation agreement, and is deemed to meet every condition under section 3-2(b) other than paragraphs (1), (3), (5), and (6) of that subsection, which apply to it.\n\n**(b) Layering.** A child enrolled in a Head Start program is enrolled under sections 2-1 and 9858d(a) of title 42, United States Code, for the hours the program furnishes, and the Head Start agency is paid the cost-model rate for those hours under this Act. Funds under the Head Start Act are available to the agency for the comprehensive services, family services, and additional hours that Act requires, and shall not be reduced, offset, or reallocated on account of any payment under this Act. Nothing in this Act alters the eligibility of any child under section 645 of the Head Start Act (42 U.S.C. 9840) or the priority of any child for Head Start services.\n\n**(c) No supplanting.** A payment under this Act to a Head Start agency shall not be treated as non-Federal share for purposes of section 640(b) of the Head Start Act (42 U.S.C. 9835(b)), and a Head Start agency's costs paid under this Act are not allowable costs under a grant under that Act."},{"anchor":"sec-2-7","heading":"\u00a7 2-7. Where a State does not carry out this title.","part":"TITLE II \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRE-KINDERGARTEN","body":"Where a State has no plan in effect under section 9858c of title 42, United States Code, or has stated that it will not carry out this title, the Secretary carries out this title in the State under track A, under section 5-5."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Participating providers.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Who may participate.** Any eligible child care provider may become a participating provider by entering into a participation agreement under section 3-2 with the lead agency. The lead agency shall enter into the agreement with every eligible child care provider that agrees to its terms, within the period set in the schedule after the provider so requests, and an agreement not executed within that period is in effect by operation of this subsection on the terms of section 3-2.\n\n**(b) Relative care.** A provider described in section 9858n(6)(B) of title 42, United States Code, is a participating provider as to the children it may serve under that subparagraph, on the conditions under section 3-2(b)(2), (3), and (5) only, at the rate set for that class of care.\n\n**(c) No cap, no selection.** The lead agency has no authority to limit the number of participating providers, to select among providers that agree to the terms, or to add a condition of participation not stated in section 3-2(b).\n\n**(d) Military child development programs.**\n\n1. **Participating by operation of law.** A military child development center, and a provider of family home day care, each within the meaning of section 1800 of title 10, United States Code, is a participating provider under this Act as to the care it furnishes in a State, without a participation agreement, and is deemed to meet every condition under section 3-2(b) other than paragraphs (1), (3), (5), and (6) of that subsection, which apply to it. It is paid the cost-model rate for the class of care it furnishes, as a licensed provider of the same setting in the same locality would be. A place with such a provider counts toward sections 3-4(c) and 3-6(a) only for a child the provider admits under chapter 88 of that title.\n2. **Federal share.** The Federal share of a payment under section 9858h(d) of title 42, United States Code, or section 2-1(c) for a covered child enrolled with a provider under this subsection is 100 percent, and no State share under section 5-4 arises from it.\n3. **Fees.** A provider under this subsection charges no fee under section 1793 of title 10, United States Code, where section 3-2(b)(1) bars the charge; that section continues to govern every other child and every other hour. The Secretary of Defense may pay, from funds available to the Department of Defense for military child development programs, all or part of the family copay of a family with a child enrolled with a provider under this subsection, and may apply financial assistance under section 1798 of that title to the family copay of a family with a child enrolled with any participating provider; no such assistance is paid to a provider for care for which it is paid the cost-model rate under this Act. An amount the Secretary of Defense pays under this paragraph is excluded from the gross income of every member of the family.\n4. **Receipts.** An amount paid under this Act to a military child development center is credited to the appropriation or nonappropriated fund that bears the cost of the care, is merged with and available for the same purposes and period as that appropriation or fund, and is not subject to section 3302(b) of title 31, United States Code. For purposes of section 1791(a) of title 10, United States Code, the child care fee receipts estimated for a fiscal year include the fees that would have been charged under section 1793 of that title for the care for which the amount is paid.\n5. **Free to exceed.** Nothing in this Act limits any standard, rate of pay, fee reduction, hour of operation, or service that the Secretary of Defense prescribes or furnishes under chapter 88 of title 10, United States Code, above what this Act requires, or the care of any child who is not a covered child; and no payment under this Act reduces, offsets, or reallocates any amount available to the Department of Defense for military child development programs.\n6. **Actions.** An action under section 7-2 against a military child development center lies against the United States in a district court of the United States, and the United States waives its immunity from such an action as to the relief under section 7-2(b).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. The participation agreement.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Terms exclusive.** The participation agreement consists of the conditions in subsection (b) and no others. A condition not stated in subsection (b) is not a condition of participation, however characterized, and an agreement purporting to add one is of no effect as to the addition.\n\n**(b) Conditions.** A participating provider \u2014\n\n1. **accepts the cost-model rate as payment in full** for the care of each child enrolled under this Act, and charges the family nothing, whether as a fee, deposit, registration or materials charge, fundraising obligation, required purchase, differential for a covered child, or under any other name, regardless of form, title, or characterization; the family copay is owed to the lead agency and not to the provider;\n2. **complies with the licensing, health and safety, group size, ratio, background check, and inspection requirements** in effect under sections 9858c(c)(2)(F) through (L) and 9858f of title 42, United States Code, as they apply to every provider of its class in the State, whether or not it participates;\n3. **reports enrollment** monthly under section 9858c(c)(2)(S) of that title, and the attendance information that section requires;\n4. **pays each child care staff member not less than parity compensation** under title IV, and certifies payment under section 4-5;\n5. **does not discriminate** as section 3-4 provides;\n6. **affords parents unlimited access** as section 9858c(c)(2)(B) of that title provides; and\n7. **ends a child's enrollment only** on the parent's request, on the child ceasing to be a covered child, on the provider ceasing to operate the class, or on a documented finding that the child's continued enrollment presents a risk to the safety of the child or other children that the provider cannot address with the supports available under section 3-7, and in every case on the notice set in the schedule.\n\n**(c) No condition on content.** No condition of participation, and no provision of this Act, requires or permits the lead agency or the Secretary to prescribe, review, or condition payment on the curriculum, instructional content, or religious character of any program a participating provider furnishes, beyond the standards and guidelines named in section 2-1(d) as they apply to every provider.\n\n**(d) No waiver, no exemption.** Neither the Secretary nor a lead agency has authority to waive, exempt any provider from, or modify as to any provider, any condition in subsection (b). A categorical difference stated in this Act for a class of provider applies to every provider in that class without regard to its religious character.\n\n**(e) Uniform.** Every condition in subsection (b) applies to every participating provider in its class identically."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Payment.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Monthly; enrollment.** Section 9858c(c)(2)(S) and (c)(4)(E) of title 42, United States Code, govern payment to a participating provider. The lead agency shall pay each participating provider the full cost-model rate for each enrolled child, without reduction for the family copay or for any amount owed by any family.\n\n**(b) Recovery from providers only.** An overpayment is recoverable from the provider paid, under section 9858c(c)(2)(S)(iii) of title 42, United States Code, and from no family, staff member, or other person.\n\n**(c) No offset against families.** No amount owed by a family, and no error in a family's copay, affects any payment to a provider."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Nondiscrimination.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Grounds.** A participating provider shall not discriminate against a child in admission, enrollment, the provision of care, or the ending of enrollment on the basis of the race, color, national origin, religion, or disability of the child or of a parent or member of the child's household, or on the basis of the child's status as a covered child.\n\n**(b) Sexual orientation and gender identity.** A participating provider shall not discriminate against a child in admission, enrollment, the provision of care, or the ending of enrollment on the basis of the sexual orientation or gender identity of the child or of a parent or member of the child's household.\n\n**(c) The access guarantee.** The lead agency shall ensure that every covered child has, within the access standard set in the schedule, a participating provider with a place for the child that does not discriminate on any ground stated in subsection (a) or (b). Where no such place exists, section 1-8(c) applies to the child and the lead agency's duty under section 3-6 arises. The duty under this subsection is a duty of the lead agency as to supply; it is not conditioned on, and its performance does not depend on, the conduct of any provider.\n\n**(d) Severability within this section.** Subsections (a), (b), and (c) are severable from one another. If subsection (b), or any application of it, is held invalid, subsections (a) and (c) continue to operate as to every ground and every child, and no court shall treat subsections (a) through (c) as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together.\n\n**(e) Relation to the 1990 Act.** Section 9858l of title 42, United States Code, is amended \u2014 (1) in subsection (a)(2)(A), by striking \"(other than a family child care provider)\"; and (2) by adding at the end the following: \"**(c) Participating providers.** For purposes of this section, a participating provider under the Child Care and Pre-K Act receives assistance under this subchapter, whether it is paid under a grant, a contract, or a certificate.**\" Subsections (a)(1), (a)(3), and (a)(4) of section 9858l of that title continue to govern employment, and section 4-6 governs their relation to title IV.\n\n**(f) Federal financial assistance.** For purposes of title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and the Age Discrimination Act of 1975, a payment under this Act to a participating provider, whether under a grant, a contract, or a certificate, shall be treated as Federal financial assistance to the provider, and section 9858n(2) of title 42, United States Code, does not apply for those purposes.\n\n**(g) No exemption authority.** Section 3-2(d) applies to this section."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Religious providers.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Participation.** No eligible child care provider is excluded from participation, from any payment, or from any grant under this Act on the basis of its religious character, affiliation, or exercise.\n\n**(b) Use of funds.** Section 9858k(a) of title 42, United States Code, continues to govern a payment under a grant or contract, and section 9858n(2) of that title continues to govern a certificate. A payment under section 9858h(d) of that title for a child enrolled with a provider the parent selected under section 1-1(b) is a payment pursuant to the parent's choice under section 9858c(c)(2)(A)(i)(II) of that title.\n\n**(c) Religious Freedom Restoration Act.** Sections 3-2, 3-4, and 4-1 through 4-5 of this Act, and the conditions under sections 9858c(c)(2)(F) through (L), 9858f, and 9858l of title 42, United States Code, as they apply to a participating provider, are not subject to chapter 21B of title 42, United States Code, and this subsection explicitly excludes the application of that chapter by reference to it as section 2000bb-3(b) of that title requires.\n\n**(d) Accommodations exclusive.** The accommodations of religious exercise available to a participating provider under this Act are those stated in section 9858l(a)(1)(B) of title 42, United States Code, section 3-2(c) of this Act, section 4-6(b) of this Act, and subsection (b) of this section, and no other. The omission of any further accommodation is a determination by Congress.\n\n**(e) No governmental function conferred.** Nothing in this Act confers on a participating provider any governmental power, function, or authority; a participating provider furnishes child care services the parent selected, and its participation, its payment, and its compliance with a condition under this Act are not the performance of a function of the United States or of any State. Nothing in this Act authorizes a State to designate a participating provider a public school, or to furnish a place under title II in the State's name through a provider that is not a local educational agency."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Supply.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Duty.** The lead agency shall ensure that, within the access standard set in the schedule, a participating provider has a place for every covered child whose parent seeks one, for the hours the parent selects, and shall publish annually, by locality and age band, the number of places, the number of covered children, and every instance under section 1-8(c), **and the time from a parent's request for a place to enrollment, at the median and at the ninetieth percentile.**\n\n**(b) Supply grants.** From the amounts appropriated under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, the lead agency shall make grants to eligible child care providers and to persons that will become eligible child care providers, for the establishment, expansion, renovation, and equipment of places, for the acquisition or construction of a facility to be used for child care services, and for operating support during the period before enrollment reaches the reasonable enrollment set in the schedule. Grants are made not less than once each fiscal year, on published criteria keyed to the shortfall under subsection (a), by locality and age band; criteria that do not state the shortfall for each locality and age band, the amount available per place, and the date by which grants will be made do not satisfy this subsection, and no criterion other than the shortfall, the amount per place, the cost of the places proposed, and the applicant's licensure or registration, or its documented path to licensure or registration, may be applied. The lead agency shall obligate for places for children under the age of three not less than the share of the grants set in the schedule.\n\n**(c) Facilities.** Section 9858d(b)(1) of title 42, United States Code, does not apply to a grant under subsection (b). A facility acquired, constructed, or renovated with a grant under subsection (b) shall be used for child care services for the period set in the schedule, and the grant is recoverable, reduced ratably for each year of such use, from a grantee that ceases to so use it. Section 9858d(b)(2) of that title applies to a grant to a sectarian agency or organization as it applies to every grantee: a facility funded under this subsection is used for child care services.\n\n**(d) Where the lead agency does not act.** Where the number under subsection (a) shows a shortfall for a locality and age band in two consecutive annual publications, the Secretary shall make grants under subsection (b) in that locality directly, from the appropriation under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, and the State share under section 5-4 is not reduced on that account.\n\n**(d-1) The wait is a shortfall too.** Where the times published under subsection (a) exceed the period set in the schedule in more than the share set in the schedule, **that is evidence that the supply in that locality is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication**, together with **the places and the amount required to meet the period**, and subsection (d) applies to it as to a shortfall in the number of places."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Supports; non-standard hours; children with disabilities.","part":"TITLE III \u2014 PARTICIPATING PROVIDERS, PAYMENT, AND CONDITIONS","body":"**(a) Adjusted rates.** The cost model under section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code, shall include rates for care during non-standard hours, for care of a child with a disability at the staffing the child's individualized family service plan or individualized education program requires, and for the specialized staff and consultation a provider requires to serve a child described in section 3-2(b)(7) without ending the enrollment.\n\n**(b) Coordination.** Nothing in this Act reduces or conditions any service under part B or part C of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act, and a place under this Act is furnished to a child with a disability in the setting the child's plan under that Act identifies."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Parity compensation.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Standard.** A participating provider shall pay each child care staff member, for each hour of work, not less than the parity wage for the staff member's role, and shall furnish the benefits component under section 4-2. The parity wage and the benefits component together are parity compensation.\n\n**(b) The parity wage.** The parity wage for a role is the wage that a public-school employee in the corresponding role in the local educational agency in which the provider is located, with the equivalent credential under section 4-3 and the same years of experience in the field, is paid under the agency's published salary schedule, converted to an hourly rate on the hours and days that schedule assumes. Where the agency's schedule does not state a corresponding role, the parity wage is the wage under the schedule of the State educational agency, and where neither states one, the wage the Secretary publishes under section 4-7 for the role and locality.\n\n**(c) Roles.** The corresponding roles are: for a lead teacher of any age group, the classroom teacher; for an assistant teacher or aide, the instructional assistant or paraprofessional; for a director or site leader, the elementary school principal or assistant principal, as the size of the program set in the schedule provides; and for any other role, the role of equivalent function in the agency, as the schedule provides.\n\n**(d) Floor, not ceiling.** Nothing in this title prevents a provider from paying more, or displaces a collective bargaining agreement that provides more.\n\n**(e) Parity in the rate.** Parity compensation for each role, at the staffing the standards under section 9858c(c)(2)(H) of title 42, United States Code, require, is an input to the cost-model rate under section 9858c(c)(4)(A)(i) of that title, and the rate is not sufficient under that section unless it funds parity compensation at that staffing.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. The benefits component.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Amount.** The benefits component for a role is the parity wage for the role multiplied by the benefits ratio of the local educational agency in which the provider is located. The benefits ratio is the agency's expenditure on employee benefits divided by its expenditure on salaries and wages, each as reported for the most recent year in the Annual Survey of School System Finances conducted by the Census Bureau for the National Center for Education Statistics, and where the agency did not report, the ratio for the State. The health, retirement, and leave portions of the benefits component are the shares of the ratio the Secretary publishes under section 4-7 from the same survey, and where not published, the shares set in the schedule.\n\n**(b) Delivery.** A participating provider satisfies subsection (a) for a child care staff member by \u2014\n\n1. **health:** contributing not less than the health portion of the benefits component to a group health plan the provider offers to the staff member; or, where the provider is an eligible employer under section 9831(d)(3)(B) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, providing it through a qualified small employer health reimbursement arrangement under section 9831(d) of that Code, which the staff member may apply to coverage obtained through an Exchange under title I of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act or under the Public Option Act, and paying as wages any part of the health portion that exceeds the limit under section 9831(d)(2)(B)(iii) of that Code;\n2. **retirement:** paying not less than the retirement portion of the benefits component as wages in addition to the parity wage, which are deducted by default and forwarded to the staff member's Prosperity Account through the payroll channel under section 3-2 of the People's Prosperity Act, at a default rate equal to that portion, subject to the staff member's right to decline or opt out under section 3-1(d) of that Act; or, where that Act is not in effect, contributing that portion to a plan described in section 401(a), 403(b), or 408 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 of which the staff member is a participant; and\n3. **leave:** furnishing paid sick, personal, and vacation leave in not less than the days per year furnished to the corresponding public-school employee, prorated to hours worked, in addition to any leave a Federal or State law provides.\n\n**(c) In the rate.** The benefits component for each role is an input to the cost-model rate under section 9858c(c)(4)(A)(i) of title 42, United States Code.\n\n**(d) Tax character.** A contribution under subsection (b)(1) is excluded from the gross income of the staff member under section 106 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, including subsection (g) of that section where furnished under section 9831(d) of that Code. Amounts under subsection (b)(2) and (3) are wages of the staff member, and a contribution under subsection (b)(2) to a plan is treated as the plan provides."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Credentials and the ladder.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Pre-kindergarten lead teacher.** The lead teacher of a class furnishing places under section 2-1 holds a bachelor's degree with a specialization in early childhood education, or a bachelor's degree and a State early childhood teaching credential, and a staff member without it who is serving as such a lead teacher on the date this title applies may continue to serve for the period set in the schedule while obtaining it, at the parity wage for the credential the staff member holds.\n\n**(b) Every other role.** For every other role, the equivalent credential for purposes of section 4-1(b) is reached by any of: a bachelor's degree as under subsection (a); the Child Development Associate credential or a State credential the State plan identifies as equivalent, together with the years of experience in the field set in the schedule; or the years of experience in the field set in the schedule, together with completion of the training under section 9858c(c)(2)(G) of title 42, United States Code. A staff member on any rung of the ladder is paid the parity wage for the corresponding public-school step, and the ladder reaches the top step of the public-school schedule for every role.\n\n**(c) Attainment funded.** From the amounts under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, the lead agency shall pay the cost of tuition, fees, assessment, and release time for a child care staff member of a participating provider to obtain a credential under subsection (a) or (b), on the staff member's enrollment in a program that leads to it, without competition and without any limit on the number of staff members so paid.\n\n**(d) No further requirement.** No credential, degree, or qualification beyond those stated in this section is a condition of any role under this Act, of any rate, or of participation, and the omission of any further requirement is a determination by Congress. Section 9858c(c)(2)(G)(iv) of title 42, United States Code, continues to apply."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Owner-operators.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Net-income target.** The cost-model rate for a family child care home shall be computed so that the owner-operator, at the reasonable enrollment set in the schedule for the home's licensed capacity, receives from the rate, after the allowable expenses of the home stated in the cost model, net income for each hour of operation not less than parity compensation for the lead-teacher role, including the benefits component, which the owner-operator applies under section 4-2(b)(1) and (2) as the owner-operator's own contributions.\n\n**(b) Assistants.** A family child care home that employs a child care staff member is subject to sections 4-1 and 4-2 as to that staff member."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Certification and recovery.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Certification.** A participating provider shall certify annually, on the date set in the State plan, that it paid each child care staff member parity compensation for the year, stating for each role the number of staff members, the wage paid, and the contributions and leave furnished under section 4-2(b), and shall retain payroll and contribution records for the period set in the schedule. A certification that does not state each of those facts for each role does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(b) Recovery.** Where a provider paid a staff member less than parity compensation, the difference, with interest, is owed to the staff member, and the lead agency shall recover it from the provider and pay it to the staff member; the lead agency may recover it by offset against payments under section 3-3. A provider that certified falsely under subsection (a) is ineligible to participate for the period set in the schedule.\n\n**(c) Staff member's action.** A child care staff member paid less than parity compensation has an action under section 7-2 against the provider for the difference, without regard to any action by the lead agency."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. Relation to other law.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"**(a) Employment nondiscrimination.** Section 9858l(a)(1), (3), and (4) of title 42, United States Code, continue to govern employment by a participating provider, and apply to every participating provider that receives assistance under the 1990 Act as amended by this Act, including a provider paid by certificate.\n\n**(b) Ministerial roles.** Nothing in this title requires a participating provider to pay parity compensation for a role that consists of leading a religious organization, conducting worship services or important religious ceremonies or rituals, or serving as a messenger or teacher of the organization's faith, as distinct from the care and education of children; and nothing in this title reaches the selection of a person for such a role.\n\n**(c) Labor law.** Nothing in this title alters the status of any person as an employee or employer under any other law, and parity compensation is a floor for purposes of every such law."},{"anchor":"sec-4-7","heading":"\u00a7 4-7. Publication.","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 THE WORKFORCE","body":"The Secretary shall publish annually, for each local educational agency and each State, the salary schedules under section 4-1(b) for each corresponding role, as the agencies publish them, the benefits ratio and its portions under section 4-2(a) from the survey that section names, and the parity wage and benefits component that result. The duty is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person. Where a schedule has not been published by the date set in the schedule to this Act, the parity wage for the year is the prior year's parity wage increased by the Middle-Class Growth Rate."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Plan contents.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 9858c(c) of title 42, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(6) The guarantee.** The State plan shall state \u2014\n>\n> **(A) the election under section 2-2 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act;**\n>\n> **(B) the cost model under paragraph (4), every input, and every rate;**\n>\n> **(C) the annual dates under paragraphs (4)(D) and (5)(E);**\n>\n> **(D) the credentials the State identifies under section 4-3(b) of that Act; and**\n>\n> **(E) the State's source of the State share under section 5-4 of that Act.**\n\n**(b) Nothing else.** No matter not stated in section 9858c(c) of title 42, United States Code, is a condition of approval of a plan or of any payment, and no rule under section 9858c(a) of that title may require, as a condition of an application or a plan, any information or assurance that section does not name.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Approval by operation of law.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"**(a) Deadline.** The Secretary shall approve or disapprove a plan, in writing on the criteria of section 9858c(c) of title 42, United States Code, only, within the period set in the schedule after its submission. **A plan not disapproved within that period is approved by operation of this subsection**, and every payment under the plan is due as if approved in writing.\n\n**(b) Disapproval.** A disapproval shall identify each provision of section 9858c(c) of title 42, United States Code, the plan fails to satisfy and the respect in which it fails; a disapproval that does not is of no effect. Disapproval of a provision of a plan does not affect any other provision, and payments under the provisions not disapproved are due.\n\n**(c) Amendment.** A State may amend its plan at any time, and an amendment is approved under subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Maintenance of effort.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"**(a) Baseline.** A State shall expend, in each fiscal year, from non-Federal sources, for child care services and pre-kindergarten for children who have not entered kindergarten, not less than the amount it so expended in the fiscal year preceding enactment, increased for each subsequent fiscal year by the Middle-Class Growth Rate. The baseline amount is the amount the State reported for that year under section 9858i of title 42, United States Code, section 418(a)(2)(C) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 618(a)(2)(C)), and its published pre-kindergarten appropriations, as the Secretary determines and publishes within the period set in the schedule after enactment; the determination is final and is not adjusted by any later report. Where the Secretary has not published a determination for a State by the end of that period, the baseline is the sum of the amounts the State reported under those provisions for that year, as reported, and no later determination alters it.\n\n**(b) Consequence.** Expenditure under subsection (a) counts toward the State share under section 5-4. Where a State's expenditure under subsection (a) is less than the baseline, the shortfall is a debt of the State to the United States, and the Secretary shall recover it by offset against the Federal share payable to the State under section 9858h(d) of title 42, United States Code, for subsequent months, without reduction of any payment to any provider."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. The State share.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"**(a) Amount.** The State share of payments under section 9858h(d) of title 42, United States Code, and section 2-1(c) is the remainder after the Federal share set in the schedule.\n\n**(b) Sources.** The State share may be met from any non-Federal source, from amounts under section 418 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 618), from amounts transferred under section 404(d) of that Act (42 U.S.C. 604(d)), and from expenditure counted under section 5-3(b).\n\n**(c) Not a condition.** The State share is an obligation of the State recoverable by offset under section 5-3(b); no payment to any provider, and no entitlement of any child, is conditioned on it, and the Secretary shall pay every provider in full whether or not the State has paid its share."},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. The Federal fallback.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"**(a) Trigger.** Where a State has no plan in effect under section 9858c of title 42, United States Code, on the date a provision of this Act first applies in the State, or thereafter has none for the period set in the schedule, or notifies the Secretary that it will not carry out this Act or any title of it, the Secretary shall carry out this Act, or that title, in the State.\n\n**(b) How.** In carrying out this Act in a State, the Secretary \u2014\n\n1. enters into participation agreements under section 3-2 with eligible child care providers in the State, and pays them under section 3-3;\n2. issues certificates under section 9858n(2) of title 42, United States Code, to parents of covered children;\n3. carries out title II under track A;\n4. makes grants under section 3-6;\n5. designates, on the request of a unit of general local government, an Indian tribe, or a consortium of either, a local lead agency to carry out any function of the lead agency in its area, and a request not acted on within the period set in the schedule is granted by operation of this paragraph; and\n6. publishes the cost model and rates under section 9858c(c)(4) of that title for the State, on the inputs that section names, from the data the Secretary holds.\n\n**(c) Funding.** Payments under this section are made from the appropriation under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, at a Federal share of 100 percent, and the State's share for the period is recoverable from the State by offset under section 5-3(b).\n\n**(d) Rates.** The Secretary shall publish, within the period set in the schedule after enactment and annually thereafter, a cost-model rate for each class of care in each State, computed under section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code; the rate applies under section 1-4(b) wherever a State has published none.\n\n**(e) Resumption.** A State may resume carrying out this Act by submitting a plan, and the Secretary's functions under this section end on the first day of the first fiscal quarter beginning after the plan takes effect; every enrollment, agreement, and grant in effect on that day continues on its terms."},{"anchor":"sec-5-6","heading":"\u00a7 5-6. Tribes.","part":"TITLE V \u2014 STATE PLANS, THE STATE SHARE, AND THE FEDERAL FALLBACK","body":"An Indian tribe or tribal organization carrying out a program under section 9858m(c) of title 42, United States Code, carries out this Act for the covered children the program serves, under the standards developed under paragraph (2)(D) of that subsection, at the Federal share stated in section 9858m(c)(7) of that title; sections 5-3 and 5-4 do not apply to it; and nothing in this Act reduces the reservation under section 9858m(a)(2) of that title."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Appropriations.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":"**(a) The guarantee.** The appropriation under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, as amended by section 1-6, funds every payment under titles I through V of this Act and under the 1990 Act for covered children, and the administrative costs of the Secretary and of each lead agency in carrying out this Act, up to the administrative percentage set in the schedule.\n\n**(b) Estimates.** The President's budget shall state for each fiscal year the amount the Secretary estimates will be paid under section 9858(a) of title 42, United States Code, identified as such; the estimate does not limit the appropriation.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. The child care entitlement to States.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 418 of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 618) is amended \u2014\n\n1. in subsection (a)(3), by striking \"there are appropriated $3,550,000,000 for each fiscal year, of which\" and inserting \"**there are appropriated for each fiscal year the amount appropriated under this paragraph for the fiscal year in which the Child Care and Pre-K Act is enacted, increased for each subsequent fiscal year by the Middle-Class Growth Rate, of which**\";\n2. by striking subsection (b)(2); and\n3. in subsection (c), by inserting before the period: \"**, and amounts so transferred are available to the lead agency for the State share under section 5-4 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act and for assistance to eligible children who are not covered children**\"."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Transfers from temporary assistance for needy families.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":"Section 404(d)(1)(B) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 604(d)(1)(B)) is amended by inserting before the period: \"**, including the State share under section 5-4 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act**\"."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. The block grant continues.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":"Amounts under section 9858(b) and 9858m of title 42, United States Code, are available for assistance to eligible children who are not covered children, for the activities under section 9858e of that title, and for the administration of the 1990 Act, and every reservation and allotment under section 9858m of that title continues to apply to them. No amount under section 9858(b) of that title is used for a payment under section 9858h(d) of that title."},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Coordination with the Internal Revenue Code.","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 FINANCING AND CONSOLIDATION","body":"**(a) Section 21.** Section 21(b)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **The term \"qualifying individual\" does not include, for any month, a dependent described in subparagraph (A) who is a covered child within the meaning of section 0-3 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act and to whom section 9858d(a)(1) of title 42, United States Code, applies in that month under section 1-8 of that Act.**\n\n**(b) Section 129.** Section 129(e)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by inserting before the period: \"**, and does not include the payment or provision of services for the care of an individual excluded from the term 'qualifying individual' by the last sentence of section 21(b)(1) for the month in which the services are provided**\".\n\n**(c) Section 45F.** Section 45F(c)(1) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(C) Participating providers.** The term \"qualified child care expenditure\" does not include any amount described in subparagraph (A)(ii) or (iii) paid or incurred with respect to a facility for any month in which the facility is operated by a participating provider within the meaning of section 0-3 of the Child Care and Pre-K Act, or under a contract with such a provider for such a month.**\n\n**(d) Family copay.** A family copay is not an employment-related expense for purposes of section 21 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and is not dependent care assistance for purposes of section 129 of that Code.\n\n**(e) Care not income.** Section 9858q of title 42, United States Code, applies to care furnished under this Act, and the value of care furnished under this Act, of a place under title II, and of any grant under section 4-3(c) to a staff member, is excluded from the gross income of the recipient for purposes of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(f) Effective date.** The amendments made by subsections (a) and (b) apply to a child from the date section 9858d(a)(1) of title 42, United States Code, first applies to the child under section 1-8, and to no earlier month.\n\n**(g) Supply grants.** A grant under section 3-6(b) is excluded from the gross income of the grantee. The basis of any property acquired, constructed, or improved with the grant is reduced by the amount of the grant so used, and no deduction or credit is allowed for any expense paid from the grant.\n\n**(h) Statutory amounts and interest not deductible.** No deduction is allowed for a statutory amount under section 7-2(b), or for interest under section 9858c(c)(2)(S)(iii) of title 42, United States Code, or section 4-5(b), paid or incurred by a provider.\n\n**(i) Recoveries.** An amount recovered under section 7-2 by a parent or a child care staff member that is an amount charged, collected, or withheld contrary to this Act is not income of the recipient. A statutory amount recovered under section 7-2(b) is gross income of the recipient and is disregarded in determining eligibility for, or the amount of benefit under, any Federal or federally assisted program."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Ministerial duties.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Duties.** The publication of the cost model, inputs, and rates under section 9858c(c)(4)(D) of title 42, United States Code; the publication of unmet demand under section 9858r of that title; the measurements under sections 2-5(b) and 3-6(a); the publications under section 4-7; the payment of a participating provider under section 3-3; the payment to a State under section 9858h(d) of that title; the execution of a participation agreement under section 3-1(a); the approval of a plan under section 5-2; and each function of the Secretary under section 5-5 are ministerial duties, and each is enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.\n\n**(b) Dates certain.** Each duty under subsection (a) is due on the date this Act or the State plan sets, and where no date is set, within 30 days after the facts on which it depends exist.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Civil action.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Who may sue.** An action lies \u2014\n\n1. by a parent, on behalf of a covered child, against a lead agency or a participating provider, for a denial, delay, reduction, conditioning, or ending of the child's entitlement under section 9858d(a) of title 42, United States Code, or section 2-1, contrary to this Act;\n2. by a parent, against a participating provider, for any amount charged contrary to section 3-2(b)(1), and against a lead agency for any family copay determined or collected contrary to section 9858c(c)(5) of that title;\n3. by a parent, on behalf of a child, against a participating provider, for discrimination contrary to section 3-4(a) or (b), and against a lead agency for a failure of the duty under section 3-4(c) or 3-6(a);\n4. by a child care staff member, against a participating provider, under section 4-5(c);\n5. by an eligible child care provider, against a lead agency, for a refusal to execute a participation agreement under section 3-1, for a payment not made under section 3-3, or for a rate not set under section 9858c(c)(4) of that title; and\n6. by any person, against a lead agency or the Secretary, for a failure to publish a record this Act requires to be published, the denial of which is an injury to the person.\n\n**(b) Relief.** The court shall award the amount owed, with interest; shall order the entitlement furnished, the enrollment restored, the agreement executed, or the record published; may enjoin the practice; and shall award, for each violation of section 3-2(b)(1), 3-4(a), 3-4(b), or 4-1, the statutory amount set in the schedule, in addition to the amount owed. Relief against a lead agency in an action under subsection (a)(1) through (3) and (5) includes the amount owed.\n\n**(c) Fees.** A prevailing plaintiff shall be awarded reasonable attorney's fees and costs. A prevailing defendant may be awarded fees only where the action was frivolous, unreasonable, or without foundation. Fees shall not be reduced in proportion to the amount recovered, and a settlement conditioned on waiver of fees is unenforceable as to the fee award.\n\n**(d) Forum.** The district courts of the United States and the courts of the States have concurrent jurisdiction of an action under this section. An action commenced in a State court may not be removed to a district court of the United States. A State court shall not decline to entertain an action under this section on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States.\n\n**(e) No compelled waiver.** An agreement to waive the right to bring an action under this section, to arbitrate such an action, or to submit it to a forum other than a court, made before the conduct giving rise to it occurred, is unenforceable as to that action, notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code. Whether this subsection renders an agreement unenforceable is determined by a court, notwithstanding any term delegating that determination to an arbitrator.\n\n**(f) Limitation.** An action under this section may be brought within four years after the violation, and a violation that continues gives rise to a new period each month.\n\n**(g) State defendants.** A State that submits a plan under section 9858c of title 42, United States Code, or accepts a payment under section 9858h(d) of that title, thereby waives its immunity from an action under this section in a court of the United States or of a State, as to the relief under subsection (b); the waiver is a condition of the payment, stated expressly, and applies to every lead agency of the State. An action under this section against an officer of a lead agency for relief other than an amount owed lies without regard to this subsection.\n\n**(h) Retaliation.** No person shall take or threaten an adverse action against a parent, child, staff member, or provider because of an action under this section, a report under this Act, or the exercise of a right this Act confers, and a person subjected to such action has an action under this section for the relief under subsection (b)."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Defenses exclusive; construction.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions, and limitations available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. The common law of the United States or of any State supplies none. No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense or immunity is a determination by Congress that it is not available.\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A defense or limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, a limitation on remedy, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) Statutory; remedial.** Each action under this Act is created by this Act, is remedial, and shall be construed to give effect to its remedial purpose. No court may decline to entertain it on the ground that an alternative remedial structure exists or that special factors counsel hesitation. Congress has considered and expressly displaces, as to this Act, the presumption against the creation of causes of action and any presumption that a remedial scheme enacted elsewhere displaces a remedy provided here.\n\n**(d) Causation; pleading.** Liability under this Act requires that the conduct be a but-for cause of the injury, and no heightened causal standard applies. A complaint that alleges the conduct, the provision violated, and the defendant's connection to it satisfies the pleading requirements for an action under this Act; knowledge, intent, and motive may be alleged generally.\n\n**(e) No exhaustion; no channeling.** No administrative remedy, complaint procedure, or review is a prerequisite to an action under this Act, and no statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to channel, preclude, or displace an action under this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act. A report, audit, finding, or sanction under section 9858g of title 42, United States Code, is not a remedy for any person and does not displace any action under this Act.\n\n**(f) Mootness.** Performance of a duty after the commencement of an action does not moot the action as to the period of non-performance and does not defeat an award of fees. A defendant asserting that a practice has ceased bears the burden of establishing that it cannot reasonably be expected to recur.\n\n**(g) Irreparable injury.** For purposes of interim relief, the loss of a place, the ending of an enrollment, and the passage of a month in which a covered child is without care contrary to this Act constitute irreparable injury."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Anti-substitution.","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Charges.** Section 3-2(b)(1) applies to any amount a participating provider, an affiliate, a parent association, or any person acting with the provider requires or solicits from a family as a condition of, or in connection with, a covered child's enrollment, whatever its stated purpose or recipient.\n\n**(b) Conditions.** Where any authority other than this Act is invoked to condition, limit, or suspend a payment, an enrollment, a rate, or a participation agreement under this Act, the conditions this Act states apply to that action, and the person invoking the other authority shall publish a statement identifying both authorities and the difference between their conditions.\n\n**(c) Rates.** A rate set under any other authority for care of a covered child is the cost-model rate under section 9858c(c)(4) of title 42, United States Code, or is of no effect."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Rules of construction.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Parental choice.** Sections 9858c(c)(2)(A) and 9858o of title 42, United States Code, apply to every place and every payment under this Act; nothing in this Act favors a grant or contract over a certificate, or a provider of any class over another, except as sections 2-3(b) and 2-5(c) expressly provide as to supply.\n\n**(b) No condition on content.** Section 3-2(c) governs every provision of this Act.\n\n**(c) Existing programs.** Nothing in this Act reduces, conditions, or displaces any service or eligibility under the Head Start Act, under part B or part C of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act, or under any Federal nutrition program, and nothing in this Act is a condition of any such service.\n\n**(d) Tribal law.** Nothing in this Act dictates the content of Tribal law; where this Act funds, protects, or recognizes a Tribal government or a Tribal program, it imposes duties only as to functions voluntarily assumed.\n\n**(e) Care is not income.** The value of any care, place, grant, or contribution furnished to an individual under this Act is excluded from gross income under the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 and is disregarded in determining eligibility for, or the amount of benefit under, any Federal or federally assisted program.\n\n**(f) Immigration.** No information furnished under this Act may be used for any purpose of the immigration laws, and no benefit under this Act is a public benefit for purposes of title IV of the Personal Responsibility and Work Opportunity Reconciliation Act of 1996 or a factor under section 212(a)(4) of the Immigration and Nationality Act.\n\n**(f-1) The rule is the public school rule.** A covered child is admitted to the entitlement under this Act on the same principle on which a child is admitted to a free public elementary education: **residence, and nothing else.** This Act ends where school begins \u2014 \u00a7 0-3(b) closes coverage on the first day of the school year in which the child may enroll in kindergarten \u2014 and it admits whom school admits. **This subsection states the ground of subsection (f) and of \u00a7 0-3(b); it does not incorporate the law of any State.** The residence of a covered child is determined under this Act, and a State rule governing enrollment, documentation, or proof of residence in its public schools neither enlarges nor narrows the entitlement.\n\n**(g) Other law preserved.** Nothing in this Act reduces any protection a child, parent, staff member, or provider has under any other law, and every requirement of this Act is a floor.\n\n**(h) No rule over the terms.** No rule, guidance, or interpretation of the Secretary or of a State may define, narrow, condition, or add to a term this Act defines, a condition this Act states, or a right this Act confers; a rule that purports to do so is of no effect as to the term, condition, or right.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Severability.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Act.** If any provision of this Act, or any amendment made by it, or any application of either, is held invalid, every other provision, amendment, and application stands. Each numbered section of this Act operates independently; no court shall treat the provisions of this Act, or of this Act together with the 1990 Act or any other Act, as an integrated scheme whose parts stand or fall together; and the invalidity of any provision shall not be the basis for any inference about any other.\n\n**(b) Within sections.** Section 3-4(d) governs section 3-4. Section 7-2(g) is severable from the remainder of section 7-2, and an action under section 7-2 against a participating provider, an officer, or the Secretary lies without regard to the validity of section 7-2(g).\n\n**(c) By defendant class.** Every provision of this Act that binds a State or a lead agency is severable, as to States, from the same provision as it binds the Secretary, a participating provider, or any other person."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Effective dates.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Enactment.** Except as this section provides, this Act takes effect on enactment.\n\n**(b) The workforce and rates.** Sections 1-4, 1-5, and 5-5(d), and title IV, apply from the first day of the first fiscal year beginning after enactment.\n\n**(c) The guarantee by age band.** Section 9858d(a)(1) of title 42, United States Code, as amended by section 1-1, and section 2-1, apply to each age band from the date set in the schedule for that band under section 1-8.\n\n**(d) The Code.** The amendments made by section 6-5 apply as that section provides."},{"anchor":"sec-9-4","heading":"\u00a7 9-4. Resources and continuity of operations.","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Permanent appropriations.** The appropriations under section 9858(a) and (b) of title 42, United States Code, as amended by section 1-6, are permanent, are not subject to apportionment delay or programmatic withholding, and are obligated as the payments they fund fall due.\n\n**(b) Continuity.** The payment of participating providers and of States under sections 3-3 and 9858h(d) of title 42, United States Code, the publications under sections 9858c(c)(4)(D) and 9858r of that title and sections 2-5(b), 3-6(a), and 4-7, and the execution of participation agreements under section 3-1 are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and the personnel performing them are excepted personnel during any such lapse.\n\n**(c) Administration.** For any function of this Act not funded by a permanent appropriation, the President's budget shall state for each fiscal year the amount necessary to perform the function fully, identified as such."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE.","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 0-3(n) full-time care, hours per week; \u00a7 9858c(c)(5)(B) seat-share denominator | 50 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 1-1(c) verification period | 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 1-1(e), \u00a7 3-2(b)(7) notice before ending an enrollment | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(5)(B) base percentage, first child | 7 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(5)(B) second-child increment | 2.5 percentage points | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(5)(B) additional-child increment | 1.5 percentage points | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(5)(C) copay floor, share of State median income | 75 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(4)(A)(ii) substitute, planning, and development inputs | 12 percent of clause (i) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(4)(A)(iv) food, supplies, equipment, transportation, administration | 18 percent of clauses (i)\u2013(iii) | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(4)(A)(v), \u00a7 2-4(b), \u00a7 4-4(a) reasonable enrollment | 85 percent of licensed capacity | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-11 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(4)(E) payment period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-12 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(2)(S)(ii) consecutive days of absence; confirmation period | 20 days; 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-13 | \u00a7 9858c(c)(2)(S)(iii), \u00a7 4-5(b) ineligibility period | 5 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-14 | \u00a7 9858h(d) Federal share, child care | 90 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-15 | \u00a7 2-1(c) Federal share, pre-kindergarten | 60 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-16 | \u00a7 1-8(a), \u00a7 9-3(c) age bands and dates | ages 3 and 4: first fiscal year beginning more than 1 year after enactment; under age 1 and age 2: the following fiscal year; age 1: the fiscal year after that | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-17 | \u00a7 1-8(c), \u00a7 3-4(c), \u00a7 3-6, \u00a7 9858r access standard | 30 minutes' travel by the means the family uses | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-18 | \u00a7 2-1(a) pre-kindergarten hours and days | 6.5 hours; 180 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-19 | \u00a7 2-2(b) election lock | 5 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-20 | \u00a7 2-3(b) community-provider floor | 50 percent of places | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-21 | \u00a7 2-5(a) infant-and-toddler supply standard | 25 places per 100 children under 3, rising 3 per year to 40 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-22 | \u00a7 3-1(a) agreement execution period | 30 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-23 | \u00a7 3-6(b) share of supply grants for children under 3 | 50 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-24 | \u00a7 3-6(c) facility use period | 15 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-25 | \u00a7 4-1(c) director role by program size | 60 or more places: principal; fewer: assistant principal | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-26 | \u00a7 4-3(a) period to obtain the lead-teacher credential | 6 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-27 | \u00a7 4-3(b) years of experience on the ladder | 3 years with a CDA; 6 years with training | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-28 | \u00a7 4-5(a) record retention | 6 years | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-29 | \u00a7 4-7 publication date | July 1 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-30 | \u00a7 5-2(a) plan approval period | 90 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-31 | \u00a7 5-3(a) baseline determination period | 180 days after enactment | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-32 | \u00a7 5-5(a) period without a plan | 180 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-33 | \u00a7 5-5(d) Federal rate publication | 270 days after enactment | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-34 | \u00a7 6-1(a) administrative percentage | 5 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-36 | \u00a7 5-5(b)(5) local lead agency designation period | 60 days | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-37 | \u00a7 4-2(a) default shares of the benefits ratio | health 55 percent; retirement 35 percent; leave 10 percent | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-35 | \u00a7 7-2(b) statutory amounts | $2,500 per charge contrary to \u00a7 3-2(b)(1); $10,000 per violation of \u00a7 3-4(a) or (b); $5,000 per staff member per year under \u00a7 4-1 | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-11"}}}};
